            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, there were a few key areas representing air leakage from your home:



** The 6" chimney &amp; 6" combustion air supply pipe for the current oil furnace (whether you use the furnace or not).



** The door into the garage &amp; the one from your master bedroom to the deck.



** There was some weird leakage from the attic into the partition wall between the main bathroom &amp; the living room - I will email you an infrared image of this.



** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls, and the attic access panel



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you can achieve about a 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 275 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.9 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12 and we discussed your interest to add more insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For this house, I have modeled another R15, bringing the total R-value close to the minimum R30 that is generally required for new construction in the NWT.



Savings: By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by a minimum R15, you could potentially save $ 814 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 26.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

The foundation of your house is 80% insulated with 2x4 R12 on the interior of the concrete walls. The other 20% represents the walls of that back room where the hot water tank is located. There is no insulation on the concrete walls in that room or in the floor above. There is a heat duct into that basement area and the room above is also heated living space, so the walls of this basement section should also be insulated. I have modeled the addition of 3" (R15) of rigid foam board insulation on the interior of the concrete walls.



Savings: By adding R15 to the uninsulated concrete basement walls, you could potentially save $ 663 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 250 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 175 litre electric hot water tank. For you future planning, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 689 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older oil furnace (estimated 82% efficiency), and you have 2 wood stoves. For the basis of this energy evaluation, NRCan has us complete the analyses based upon the primary heating system for the house - which is your oil furnace. I have performed savings calculations within the "Additional Comments" section of this report to estimate the savings (net of fuel savings and a basic self-harvested wood rate of $100/cord) associated with 75% of your annual heating being supplied by the 2 wood stoves.



In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from the oil furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. The high efficiency motor will be great for distributing the heat from your woodstove throughout the house.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 830 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 44.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Wood Stoves Supplemental Heat



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency furnace and a basic self-harvested wood rate of $100/cord) if you were to use your 2 wood stoves for about 75% of the heating season and use the high efficiency blower motor of the new furnace to circulate the wood heat throughout the house.



Savings: By using your 2 wood stoves for 75% of the heating season, you could potentially save $ 4,528 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane furnace.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - two bathroom fans

        - there is a clothes dryer (however the vent was 100% plugged during my visit) - I let Sarah know



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan in the lower level bathroom that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.0 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not estimated to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and the woodstove in the basement vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. The wood stove on the upper level appears to have a dedicated outside combustion air connected.



During my assessment of your home, I did not see any smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.25 per kWh

Oil  - $1.17 per litre 

Propane - $0.87 per litre

Wood - $242 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $8.49 per bag, $288.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climah transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
